  "text": "## Deep context: Rebate Measures\nRoute: /rebate-measures\nSummary: The rebate engine's source of truth — per measure code, baseline wattage, proposed wattage, dollars per unit, caps. Tweak a row, every audit's math updates.\n\n**What it is** — The line-item rebate table — one row per measure code per utility program per source year. Drives every rebate calculation Lenard performs.\n\n**How it works** — rebate_measures table (utility_program_id, source_year, measure_code, baseline_w, proposed_w, dollars_per_unit, cap_per_unit, cap_per_project). Lookup at audit time: match fixture type → measure_code → apply cap math. Custom-calc fallback uses utility_programs.kwh_rate when no measure matches.\n\n**Examples**\n- Audit has 240 fixtures of type 2x4 LED → matches RMP measure code LF-LED-2X4-PROP\n- Per-unit $80, cap-per-unit $80 → $80 × 240 = $19,200, no cap applied\n- Project cap $25k → applied if total exceeds\n\n**Gotchas (warn the user about these)**\n- Caps apply per-unit AND per-project. The engine evaluates both and uses the lower.\n- measure_code must match the utility's exact spelling for the PDF auto-fill to work.\n\n**FAQs**\n- Q: Can I run the math without a measure code match?\n  A: Yes — custom-calc mode kicks in when no measure matches. Uses kWh-saved × utility rate × cap.\n- Q: How do I know the measures are up to date?\n  A: Each program has a source_year. The page badges old years as stale. You can pull the latest from the utility PDF anytime.\n\n**Setup steps**\n1. Open Rebate Measures — Filter by utility program. Confirm the count matches what the utility publishes (RMP Wattsmart ~80 measures, SRP Custom ~40).\n2. Spot-check a few rows — Pick a common fixture (2x4 LED panel). Confirm baseline + proposed wattage + dollars per unit against the utility PDF.\n3. Add a custom measure — If you need a measure the utility does not publish (your distributor SKU), click + Add Measure. Set baseline / proposed / $ / cap.\n4. New year, new measures — When the utility publishes a new year, load the new measures. Old audits stay locked to their source_year — no number drift."
